  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Colorado for FY2026 to date total $9.3B. That total comes from 15,725 awards and an average award size of $592K. Obligations are down 42.2% year over year. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer with $5.6B in obligations, and two vendors, SIERRA NEVADA COMPANY, LLC and LOCKHEED MARTIN CORP, account for $1.8B and $1.6B respectively. Key industries include Aircraft Manufacturing at $1.5B and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) at $1.1B. Other sizable sectors are Engineering Services at $800M and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $663M. FY2026 to date is a partial fiscal-year snapshot and follows five prior full fiscal years that ranged from $12.8B in FY21 to $16.1B in FY25. The concentration in defense buyers and aircraft and missile manufacturing stands out, while the mix of R&D and services shows continuing demand for technical and engineering work in the state.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal spending in Colorado?",
        "body": "Spending is highly concentrated. The Department of Defense accounts for $5.6B of the $9.3B total. Two vendors, SIERRA NEVADA COMPANY, LLC and LOCKHEED MARTIN CORP, account for $1.8B and $1.6B respectively, so prime awards are clustered among a few large contractors."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What sectors and vendors dominate the market?",
        "body": "Aircraft Manufacturing leads with $1.5B, followed by large R&D categories at $1.1B and $493M. Engineering Services and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing are also large at $800M and $663M. The top vendors include SIERRA NEVADA COMPANY, LLC at $1.8B and LOCKHEED MARTIN CORP at $1.6B."
      }
    ]
  },
